# Prepare the formula for a logistic GLMM with one fixed effect and random
intercept
formula <- y ~ 1 + x + f(subject, model = "iid", constr = TRUE,
                         hyper = list(prec = list(prior = "pc.prec",
                                                  param = c(0.5, 0.01))))

# Fit the model using INLA

inla_fit <- inla(
  formula,
  family = "binomial",

  control.fixed = list(prec.intercept = 1),

  data = simulated_data,
  control.predictor = list(link = 1)
)

On Thu, 2024-11-07 at 06:54 +0000, Finn Lindgren wrote:
> Hi,
> Not entirely clear without info about the data size etc if this is the reason,
> but your model actually has _two_ fixed effects, as R’s formula system adds an
> automatic intercept terms unless you tell it not to do that. You can remove
> that with e.g.
>   Y~ 0+ x+…
> In the formula specification.
>
> But also, if the data doesn’t need the subject level iid effect, due to the
> variability being sufficiently explained by the common intercept and x-
> covariate effect and the inherent binomial variability, then that can explain
> why it estimates a large precision.
> Since your model has binary data, this can easily happen if the data size is
> small.
>
> Finn
