Subject: Questions About Large Differences Between DIC and WAIC
Message-ID: <8jeIC5iQd_E.35quFOrHAAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/8jeIC5iQd_E/m/35quFOrHAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

 Hello everyone,

I am a new user on the forum and I am just starting to use INLA. I am
working on spatiotemporal models and I have a question that I hope
you can help me solve.

I am currently fitting spatiotemporal models with interaction random
effects following the Knorr-Held scheme, that is, using the Kronecker
product to combine the spatial and temporal effect structure matrices
(types I, II, III or IV). My idea is to fit models with various
structures for both interaction effects and direct effects of time
(iid, RW1, RW2) and space (LCAR, ICAR, etc.), comparing them using
fit measures such as DIC and WAIC, and also using predictive measures
such as CPO.

When comparing the models, I expected to find close values ​​between
DIC and WAIC, since both are based on model likelihood, with slight
differences arising from their definitions. However, I have been
surprised to find very large differences in several models, on the
order of 10^3. This makes model selection difficult and makes me
wonder if I am making a mistake in the model specification or
violating one of its assumptions.

On the forum I found a comment that said:

"However, sometimes you get a massive difference in DIC or WAIC,
like 10000. That gives you a strong hint that one of the models are
better."

However, I would like to understand more in depth why these
differences occur and how to interpret them.

To give more context, I'm sharing an example of the code I'm using to
fit the models in INLA:

sdunif = "expression:

logdens=-log_precision/2;

return(logdens)"

lunif = "expression:

a = 1;

b = 1;

beta = exp(theta)/(1+exp(theta));

logdens =
lgamma(a+b)-lgamma(a)-lgamma(b)+(a-1)*log(beta)+(b-1)*log(1-beta);

log_jacobian = log(beta*(1-beta));

return(logdens+log_jacobian)"

g <- inla.read.graph("...")

Qs <- matrix(0, g$n, g$n)

for (i in 1:g$n) {

 Qs[i, i] = g$nnbs[[i]]

 Qs[i, g$nbs[[i]]] = -1

}

R.Leroux <- diag(dim(Qs)[1]) - Qs

mod.st0 <- inla(O ~ f(ID_muni, model = "generic1",
Cmatrix = R.Leroux, constr = TRUE,

 hyper = list(prec = list(prior = sdunif), beta = list(prior = lunif,
initial = 0))) +

 f(ID_year, model = "iid", constr = TRUE,

 hyper = list(prec = list(prior = "pc.prec", param = c(1,
0.01)))),

 family = "poisson", data = Data_sp, E = E,

 control.predictor
= list(compute = TRUE, cdf = c(log(1))),

 control.fixed = list(mean.intercept = 0, prec.intercept = 0.001),

 control.compute = list(dic = TRUE, waic = TRUE, cpo = TRUE),

 control.inla = list(strategy = "simplified.laplace"),

 num.threads = 4)

Results obtained for this model:

Specifically, with this model you obtain:

Deviance Information Criterion (DIC)...............: 12481.61

Deviance Information Criterion (DIC, saturated) ....: 4186.50

Effective number of parameters .....................: -758.54

Watanabe-Akaike information criterion (WAIC) ...: 14445.87

Effective number of parameters.................: 667.39

Marginal log-Likelihood: -7648.46

I would really appreciate any explanation about these differences.

Thanks in advance to everyone and best regards.

Javier

